course: dictionary
lesson: 9488
title: Steamship
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A steamship is a large ship driven by a steam engine. The engine burns coal or oil to boil water and make steam, and the steam pushes the ship along. You can see the two parts of the word: steam and ship.
explain: Before steamships, most big ships moved with wind filling their sails. Steamships replaced these sailing ships, so journeys across the sea became faster and more reliable. The sentence «The end-poem is a riddle about steamship companies of 1902» shows that steamships were run by companies, which were businesses that owned and ran them.
explain: Context gives more clues. «Travel by steamship» means travel on a ship driven by steam. «A steamship company» means a business that runs steamships. Check by swapping in your meaning: "travel on a ship driven by steam" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: A steamship is a large ship driven by a steam engine.
fact: The engine burns coal or oil to boil water and make steam.
fact: Steamships replaced sailing ships, making sea journeys faster and more reliable.
fact: Steamships helped people emigrate, which means move to live in another country, such as America, Canada, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: The Titanic was a steamship.
exercise: A steamship is driven by what? | *A steam engine | A horse and cart | A pair of oars | Wind filling its sails
exercise: What does a steamship burn to boil water? | *Coal or oil | Sand | Wood and leaves | Paper
exercise: Steamships replaced sailing ships. What did they make faster and more reliable? | *Sea journeys | Painting | Cooking | Reading
test: What does «a steamship company» most likely mean? | A shop that sells steam | A club for sailing fans | A ship with no engine | *A business that runs steamships
test: Which of these was a steamship? | A rowing boat | A kayak | *The Titanic | A paper boat
exam: Why did steamships help people emigrate? | Because they only sailed on small rivers | *Because they made sea journeys faster and more reliable than sailing ships | Because they were made of paper and floated | Because they had no engines and drifted with the tide
